Chapter: CrossPortRefused

 The pin for the cross-lane port refusal (BACKLOG 3.20). The x86 port
 builtins and the codex-vm GPU behind gpu-in and gpu-out have no
 counterpart on the ARM64 and RISC-V Renode boards, so those plugs
 refuse the compile with one [UNSUPPORTED] report per call, by design,
 instead of emitting a silently broken call. The .cross-refusal
 sidecar makes the cross battery demand that refusal: on a cross lane
 this test must FAIL to compile, naming both builtins, and on x86-64
 it must compile and run normally, because there the ports are real.
 A refusal nobody tests is a guard nobody wired; this is the wire.

 The two calls cover the two refused surfaces, one Device.Port and one
 Gpu.Compute, through the one shared refusal helper in each plug. Port
 128 is the POST diagnostic port and 1039 is a GPU port with no OUT
 handler, so on x86 both writes are absorbed and the results are
 ignored; the printed line is the only output either lane compares.

Section: Main

  touch-port : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  touch-port (p) = port-out-byte p 0

  touch-gpu : Integer -> [Gpu.Compute] Integer
  touch-gpu (p) = gpu-out p 0

  opening : [Console, Device.Port, Gpu.Compute] Nothing = act
   a <- touch-port 128
   b <- touch-gpu 1039
   print-line-uni "port-refusal-probe"
  end
